 # Erectile Prosthesis (Penile Implant) After Gender-Affirming Phalloplasty
 
-**Penile implant placement after gender-affirming phalloplasty** is a complex, high-risk procedure that gives transmasculine individuals a rigid neophallus for penetrative intercourse. When a patient chooses an implant, placement is commonly a later stage of masculinizing genital reconstruction.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup> Programs commonly wait 9–12 months after phalloplasty, until the flap has healed and vascular pedicle anatomy and reliable micturition have been confirmed.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[5]](#ref5)</sup> In Rooker’s 2019 review, 22 of 23 included retrospective reports were tabulated; among those selected reports, 792/1,056 (75.0%) phalloplasty patients received a prosthesis. This within-review fraction does not estimate implant uptake among all phalloplasty patients.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> Of these, 36.2% (287/792) had a prosthesis complication requiring additional surgery; 60.0% (305/508) had the original implant at follow-up (mean 2.6 years among 433 patients in retention studies reporting mean follow-up), and 83.9% (193/230) achieved penetration in studies reporting that outcome.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+**Penile implant placement after gender-affirming phalloplasty** is a complex, high-risk procedure that gives transmasculine individuals a rigid neophallus for penetrative intercourse. When a patient chooses an implant, placement is commonly a later stage of masculinizing genital reconstruction.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup> Programs commonly wait 9–12 months after phalloplasty, until the flap has healed and vascular pedicle anatomy and reliable micturition have been confirmed.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[5]](#ref5)</sup> In Rooker’s 2019 review, 22 of 23 included retrospective reports were tabulated; among those selected reports, 792/1,056 (75.0%) phalloplasty patients received a prosthesis.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> Of these, 36.2% (287/792) had a prosthesis complication requiring additional surgery; 60.0% (305/508) had the original implant at follow-up (mean 2.6 years among 433 patients in retention studies reporting mean follow-up), and 83.9% (193/230) achieved penetration in studies reporting that outcome.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
 For the cohort-level framework, see [Masculinizing Gender-Affirming Surgery](/docs/surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/masculinizing-surgery). For prior reconstruction, see the phalloplasty pages in 04k. [Gender-Affirming Hysterectomy](/docs/surgical-techniques/04k-gender-affirming-surgery/gender-affirming-hysterectomy) is pathway-dependent rather than a prerequisite for every penile implant.<sup>[[35]](#ref35)</sup>
 
