 # Pelvic Fracture Urethral Injury (PFUI)
 
-**Pelvic fracture urethral injury (PFUI)** is disruption of urethral continuity near the bulbomembranous junction after high-energy pelvic ring trauma; in a 10-cadaver autopsy series, 7 disruptions were distal to the external sphincter.<sup>[[69]](#ref69)</sup> The ACS guideline cites urethral injury in up to 5% of men with anterior pelvic ring fractures; the EAU reports that falls from height account for 6–25% of PFUIs in cited series.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[37]](#ref37)</sup> The injury results from distraction of the membranous urethra away from the bulbar urethra as the pelvic ring disrupts. For most men with complete posterior disruption, the standard initial management is suprapubic tube (SPT) placement, followed by delayed anastomotic urethroplasty (typically 3–6 months later). Reported outcomes vary by procedure and endpoint: in a 122-patient PFUI cohort, 111 (91%) needed no repeat intervention at a median 7-month follow-up; a separate series reported 95% success after perineal and 97% after transpubic anastomotic urethroplasty.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
+**Pelvic fracture urethral injury (PFUI)** is disruption of urethral continuity near the bulbomembranous junction after high-energy pelvic ring trauma; in a 10-cadaver autopsy series, 7 disruptions were distal to the external sphincter.<sup>[[69]](#ref69)</sup> The ACS guideline cites urethral injury in up to 5% of men with anterior pelvic ring fractures; the EAU reports that falls from height account for 6–25% of PFUIs in cited series.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[37]](#ref37)</sup> The injury results from distraction of the membranous urethra away from the bulbar urethra as the pelvic ring disrupts. For most men with complete posterior disruption, the standard initial management is suprapubic tube (SPT) placement, followed by delayed anastomotic urethroplasty (typically 3–6 months later).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
 
 For obliterative post-traumatic posterior stenosis, the 2026 EAU Urethral Strictures guideline weakly recommends open reconstruction only in high-volume centers, strongly recommends progressive perineal excision and primary anastomosis, and strongly recommends against endoscopic treatment; cut-to-the-light risks a false passage toward the bladder base or rectum. For short (&lt;1.5 cm) nonobliterative stenosis, it weakly recommends one endoluminal attempt.<sup>[[64]](#ref64)</sup> See the limitations of [Core-Through Urethrotomy](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/core-through-urethrotomy). For short bulbar EPA, see [Excision and Primary Anastomosis](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/excision-primary-anastomosis).
 
